{
  "id": "80524",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Nuclear Reactions and Equations Summary",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "hmnjgg6mjn5f8zd",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "92k6inj4wjb26aw",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "An atomic nucleus consists of protons and neutrons, collectively called nucleons. Although protons repel each other, the nucleus is held tightly together by a short-range but very strong force called the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "strong nuclear force"
              },
              {
                "text": ". A nucleus has less mass than the total mass of its constituent nucleons. This “missing” mass is the mass defect, which has been converted into the binding energy that holds the nucleus together according to Einstein’s mass–energy equivalence equation, "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "E"
              },
              {
                "text": " = "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "mc"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": ". Of the many nuclides that exist, only a small number are stable. These stable nuclides occupy a narrow band of stability on a graph of the number of protons versus the number of neutrons. The binding energy per nucleon is largest for the elements with mass numbers near 56; these are the most stable nuclei."
              }
            ],
            "id": "gdtwhlrleulhl13",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Nuclei can undergo reactions that change their number of protons, number of neutrons, or energy state. Many different particles can be involved in nuclear reactions. The most common are protons, neutrons, positrons (which are positively charged electrons), alpha ("
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2920232138",
                "src": "\\alpha",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": ") particles (which are high-energy helium nuclei), beta ("
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "id": "140474339",
                "src": "\\beta",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": ") particles (which are high-energy electrons), and gamma ("
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "id": "4276360462",
                "src": "\\gamma",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": ") rays (which compose high-energy electromagnetic radiation). As with chemical reactions, nuclear reactions are always balanced. When a nuclear reaction occurs, the total mass (number) and the total charge remain unchanged."
              }
            ],
            "id": "5hs1fol6187h31s",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Nuclei with unstable neutron-to-proton (n:p) ratios undergo spontaneous radioactive decay. The most common types of radioactivity are "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1162724345",
                "src": "\\alpha",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " decay, "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2046696729",
                "src": "\\beta",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " decay, "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1173848234",
                "src": "\\gamma",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " emission, positron emission, and electron capture. Nuclear reactions also often involve "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "id": "815721956",
                "src": "\\gamma",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " rays, and some nuclei decay by electron capture. Each of these decay modes leads to the formation of a new nucleus with a more stable n:p ratio. Some substances undergo radioactive decay series, proceeding through multiple decays before ending in a stable isotope."
              }
            ],
            "id": "nm1utk21xy1kxgw",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "numbofgoysw0p4a",
